@title Teach the Process, Not the Facility
@audience simulation and specialist-facility teams, programme leads, educational developers
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [🔁/法 🏗️/器]
@see or3/scenarios-from-lived-experience, or/living-materials-stewardship

! conclusion: Teach the Process, Not the Facility

  + context: A specialist team has built something expensive and good, and colleagues elsewhere want it too.

  + IF:
    Your institution has a capability that took real capital and expertise to establish — a simulation suite, a studio, a lab-based teaching method — and other programmes are asking for access to what it produces.

  + HOWEVER:
    There are only two obvious answers and both are wrong. Running everyone's sessions for them makes the specialist team a bottleneck that scales by burnout; telling each School to build its own duplicates the capital investment for no gain. Meanwhile what colleagues actually copy is the visible artefact — the scenario, the exercise — and not the reason it works.

  + THEN:
    Separate the facility from the method, and teach the method. Run short faculty-training courses on the underlying process — how to design an activity, how to run it safely, and above all how to debrief it — and mentor colleagues through building their own. Keep the facility as a shared service for the cases that genuinely need it. Say explicitly that the transferable asset is the process: "it's not about the scenarios so much as the inquiry-based learning process."

  + BECAUSE:
    The process is the part that generalises and the part that is usually missing: home-made versions tend to reproduce the activity and skip the debrief, which is where the learning actually happens. Teaching it converts a scarce facility into distributed capability without duplicating the spend, and lets the method cross into domains the original team does not serve — health into faculty and teacher training, for instance.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Ask the specialist team for a faculty-training run rather than for session delivery.]
    next[For advice on adapting inquiry-based simulation and structured debriefing to your own programme, contact the SimLab team in the Faculty of Health and Life Sciences.]
